---
import Guide from "../../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import CodeGroup from "../../../components/CodeGroup.astro"
import DocLink from "../../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Code from "../../../components/Code.astro"
import { pickAndChooseCss } from "../../../utils/getting-started"
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Getting started</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    OPUI ships first-class Svelte 5 components. Install the package, import what
    you need, and you're set.
  </Fragment>

  <section>
    <h2 id="installation">Install via NPM</h2>
    <p>
      The components ship as uncompiled Svelte components (<code>.svelte</code> and
      <code>.ts</code>), so your build has to compile them. They need Svelte
      5.29 or later. Any setup with Svelte support works, for example SvelteKit,
      Vite with <code>@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte</code>, or Astro with <code
        >@astrojs/svelte</code
      >.
    </p>
    <CodeGroup
      tabs={[
        {
          title: "pnpm",
          code: "pnpm add opui-css open-props svelte -S",
          lang: "sh",
        },
        {
          title: "npm",
          code: "npm install opui-css open-props svelte -S",
          lang: "sh",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="css">Import the CSS</h2>
    <p>
      Svelte components ship markup only - the CSS still has to be imported
      once, in your root layout or the entry file that mounts your app. Import
      everything:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`<!-- src/routes/+layout.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  import "opui-css/css/imports.css"

  let { children } = $props()
</script>

{@render children()}`}
      lang="svelte"
    />
    <p>
      Or pick and choose in a CSS file, and import that file in your layout
      instead:
    </p>
    <Code code={`/* src/styles/main.css */\n${pickAndChooseCss}`} lang="css" />
    <Code
      code={`<!-- src/routes/+layout.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  import "../styles/main.css"

  let { children } = $props()
</script>

{@render children()}`}
      lang="svelte"
    />
    <p>
      Import <code>layers.css</code> first and <code>utils.css</code> last, it holds
      <code>.ui-sr-only</code> and the motion classes. <code>choice.css</code> goes
      before <code>checkbox.css</code>, <code>radio.css</code> and <code
        >switch.css</code
      >. Import <code>opui-css/css/components.css</code> instead of single component
      files to get every component.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="usage">How to use</h2>
    <Code
      code={`<script lang="ts">
  import { Button } from "opui-css/svelte"
</script>

<Button variant="filled">Click me</Button>`}
      lang="svelte"
    />
    <p>
      Containers take parts, such as <code>CardHeader</code> and <code
        >DialogActions</code
      >. Other slots are snippets: the default slot is <code>children</code> and named
      slots are camelCased, such as <code>endText</code>. <code>TextField</code
      >, <code>Textarea</code>, <code>Select</code> and <code>Range</code> support
      <code>bind:value</code>. <code>Checkbox</code> and <code>Switch</code> support
      <code>bind:checked</code> and <code>bind:group</code>, and <code
        >Radio</code
      > supports <code>bind:group</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <DocLink href="/guide/concepts">Concepts</DocLink> explains how props map to
      classes, the cascade layers and what needs JavaScript. Then browse the <DocLink
        href="/components">components</DocLink
      >. <DocLink href="/guide/theming">Theming</DocLink> covers colors, density,
      motion and contrast.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="ssr">Server rendering</h2>
    <p>
      Without hydration (SvelteKit with <code>csr = false</code>, or Astro with <code
        >@astrojs/svelte</code
      > and no <code>client:*</code> directive), every component renders complete
      HTML. The native controls work and submit with their form. A few things only
      update on the client:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/checkbox#indeterminate">Checkbox</DocLink>
        <code>indeterminate</code>: it's a DOM property, so the box looks
        unchecked. Or call <code>activateIndeterminate()</code> from <code
          >opui-css/css/js/checkbox.js</code
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/range#value">Range</DocLink> value: the <code
          >&lt;output&gt;</code
        > doesn't follow the thumb. The track fill is CSS, so it's fine.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>bind:</code> on <DocLink href="/components/checkbox"
          >Checkbox</DocLink
        >, <DocLink href="/components/radio">Radio</DocLink>, <DocLink
          href="/components/range">Range</DocLink
        >, <DocLink href="/components/select">Select</DocLink>, <DocLink
          href="/components/switch">Switch</DocLink
        >, <DocLink href="/components/text-field">Text field</DocLink> and <DocLink
          href="/components/textarea">Textarea</DocLink
        >: the bound state stays put. The native value still changes and
        submits.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Hydrate those (<code>client:load</code> in Astro) or keep client-side rendering
      on. Component pages say what needs hydration next to the browser support chips,
      and the API tables mark each prop with a crossed-out server icon.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
